1.AsciiArtAlgorithm runs the process: it pads and splits the image using ImagePadderAndSplitter,
calculates brightness with BrightnessCalculator, and matches brightness to characters using SubImgCharMatcher.
Image handles loading and accessing pixel data. BrightnessCalculator avoids repeated calculations using caching,
and applies the Memento pattern to save and restore previous brightness states.
SubImgCharMatcher maps brightness values to characters, using CharBrightnessCache to store and normalize brightness.
It also supports rounding up, down, or to the nearest match, depending on user input.
//TODO להוסיף על נחלקות של חלק ב

2. HashMap<Character, Double>, used in CharBrightnessCache.
Provides constant-time O(1) average access, insertion, and removal.
This is crucial for fast brightness-to-character mapping during runtime.
Memory: Proportional to the size of the character set (typically small).

TreeSet<Character>, used in CharBrightnessCache.
Guarantees consistent order and enables predictable iteration,
which is important when brightness values are closely matched.
Time: O(logn) for insertion, deletion, and lookup.
Memory: Proportional to the size of the character set

HashMap<String, Double>, used in BrightnessCalculator.
Avoids redundant brightness calculations for identical sub-images,
significantly improving performance.
Time: O(1) average per access
Memory: Scales with the number of distinct sub-images encountered

3.

4. We added the methods getCharByBrightnessUp(double brightness) and getCharByBrightnessDown(double brightness)
to SubImgCharMatcher class to support the rounding options required in the assignment.
The actual logic for finding the character is inside CharBrightnessCache,
which is a helper class we use to store and calculate brightness values.
But we didn’t want to expose that class directly. Instead, SubImgCharMatcher serves as a wrapper
(or façade) class that exposes a clean public API for the rest of the system.
These methods were added to this class to preserve encapsulation and keep the internal
details of brightness caching hidden.

5.We modified the saveImage method in the Image class because the saved image was flipped,
the rows and columns were mixed up. The original loop accessed the pixel array in a way
that the image didn’t match what was shown in the program.
We fixed this by correcting the loop order and array access, so the saved image
now keeps the correct orientation.